.TH NOTIFYD 1 2026-11-28 "notifyd 0.52.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
notifyd \- desktop notification daemon for X11 with rules, presets and a colorful log
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B notifyd daemon
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-renderer
.BR gtk | none ]
.RB [ \-\-replace ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-format
.BR pretty | json ]
.RB [ \-\-color
.BR auto | always | never ]
.RB [ \-v | \-q ]
.br
.B notifyd send
.RI [ options ]
.I summary
.RI [ body ]
.br
.B notifyd\-send
.RI [ options ]
.I summary
.RI [ body ]
.br
.B notifyd ctl
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B notifyd presets
.RB [ \-\-pick | \-\-names ]
.br
.B notifyd icons
.RI [ pattern ]
.RB [ \-\-context
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-theme
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-names | \-\-pick | \-\-contexts | \-\-preview
.IR name ]
.br
.B notifyd import
.BR deadd " [" \-\-yml
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-css
.IR path ]
|
.BR dunst " [" \-\-rc
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B notifyd check
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.br
.B notifyd rules
.RB [ \-c
.IR path ]
.RI [ fields ]
.br
.B notifyd completion
.BR zsh | bash | fish
.br
.B notifyd help
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B notifyd version
.RB | " \-V" | " \-\-version"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B notifyd
is a server for the freedesktop.org Desktop Notifications specification (1.2): it owns
.B org.freedesktop.Notifications
on the session bus and draws the notifications applications send as translucent GTK3 popups
on X11. It combines deadd\-notification\-center's look (the default
.B deadd
preset: a black card at 0.8 opacity, no frame, the shadow left to the compositor) with dunst's
rule engine and command line control.
.PP
Replacing a notification by id keeps the id; the
.B x\-dunst\-stack\-tag
and
.B x\-canonical\-private\-synchronous
hints replace the previous notification with the same app and tag. The
.B value
hint draws a progress bar, actions become buttons, and a notification under the pointer does not
expire. Summary and body take Pango markup and <a href> links (a click opens http, https and mailto links only; href="action:KEY" invokes the notification's action KEY).
.PP
Every event (shown, replaced, waiting, suppressed, expired, dismissed, closed, action, pause,
reload) is written to standard error as an aligned line colored by urgency and event, or as JSON
lines, and broadcast on D\-Bus for
.BR "notifyd ctl watch" .
.PP
Every command takes
.BR \-h / \-\-help ,
except
.BR send ,
where
.B \-h
is
.B \-\-hint
as in
.BR notify\-send (1).
.SH COMMANDS
.SS daemon
Run the daemon in the foreground. It reloads its config when the file or a user preset changes, on
SIGHUP, or on
.BR "notifyd ctl reload" ;
open popups and the center take the new look at once. A broken file is shown as a notification until
it is fixed, and the previous config kept.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Config file (default
.IR ~/.config/notifyd/config.yaml ).
.TP
.BR \-\-renderer " gtk|none"
.B none
draws nothing and only logs, for headless use and debugging.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Take the bus name from the daemon that owns it (if it allows replacement). Without it, notifyd
exits with status 1 and names the daemon that owns the name.
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-format " pretty|json"
Event log format (default: the config's
.BR log.format ,
pretty).
.TP
.BR \-\-color " auto|always|never"
Color the log; auto colors a terminal unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log debug events too.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Log only warnings and errors.
.SS send
.B notifyd send
and
.B notifyd\-send
(the same, as its own command) send a notification through whichever daemon owns the notification
name. They take
.BR notify\-send (1)'s
flags and notify\-send.py's, plus flags for the hints notifyd and dunst understand. With actions
they wait, print the key of the action chosen and exit.
.B \-h
is
.BR \-\-hint ,
as in notify\-send; help is
.B \-\-help
only. In the summary and body,
.BR \(rsn ,
.B \(rst
and
.B \(rs\(rs
become a line break, a tab and a backslash, as notify\-send.py does.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-app\-name " \fIname\fR"
The sending application; rules match on it. Default notifyd\-send.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-urgency " low|normal|critical"
Urgency.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-expire\-time " \fIms\fR"
How long to show it; 0 until closed. A rule's timeout wins over it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-icon " \fIicon\fR"
App icon: an icon name or a path.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-category " \fItype\fR"
The category hint, e.g. email.arrived.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-transient
Keep it out of history.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-hint " \fItype\fB:\fIname\fB:\fIvalue\fR"
An extra hint; type is int, double, string, byte, boolean or variant (GVariant text, e.g.
.BR "variant:x:<int32 5>" ).
Repeatable;
.B \-\-hint
also takes several values, as in notify\-send.py.
.TP
.BR \-\-tag " \fItag\fR"
Stack tag: replaces the last notification with this app and tag.
.TP
.BR \-\-image " \fIpath\fR"
A picture shown in the notification: a file (sent as a file:// image\-path hint), a URI or an icon
name.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress " \fIn\fR"
A progress bar at
.I n
percent.
.TP
.BR \-\-title\-end " \fImarkup\fR"
The x\-notifyd\-title\-end hint, shown at the right end of the title (notifyd only).
.TP
.BR \-\-desktop\-entry " \fIname\fR"
The sender's .desktop file, without .desktop: gives the icon, and rules match it.
.TP
.BR \-\-sound\-name " \fIname\fR" ", " \-\-sound\-file " \fIpath\fR" ", " \-\-suppress\-sound
A freedesktop sound name or a sound file to play, or ask for no sound.
.TP
.B \-\-resident
It stays open after an action is chosen.
.TP
.B \-\-action\-icons
Each action's
.I name
is an icon name, drawn as an icon button with the action's text as its tooltip.
.TP
.BR \-\-fg ", " \-\-bg ", " \-\-frame ", " \-\-highlight " \fIcolor\fR"
dunst's fgcolor, bgcolor, frcolor and hlcolor hints: the text, background, border and progress bar
color of this notification. fgcolor colors all its text, over the preset's part and urgency colors,
unless a matching rule sets foreground.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-action " [\fIname\fB=\fR]\fItext\fR"
A button (also
.IB name : text\fR);
waits and prints the name chosen. Repeatable;
.B \-\-action
also takes several
.IB name : text
values.
.TP
.BR \-\-dont\-quit\-on\-action ", " \-\-dontQuitOnAction
After an action, keep waiting (printing each one chosen) until the notification closes.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wait
Wait until it is closed.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-replace\-id ", " \-\-replaces\-id " \fIid\fR"
Replace notification
.IR id .
.TP
.BR \-\-replace\-name ", " \-\-replaces\-process " \fIname\fR"
Replace the last notification sent with this
.IR name ;
ids are kept in
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/notifyd/send\-ids.json .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-print\-id
Print the notification id.
.TP
.BR \-\-close " \fIid\fR"
Close notification
.I id
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-\-completion " zsh|bash|fish"
.RB ( notifyd\-send
only) Print its completion script.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help.
.SS ctl
Control the running daemon over its
.B io.github.njhoffman.Notifyd
interface.
.TP
.B status
Paused state, how many are shown, waiting and held back, the preset and the config file.
.TP
.BR list " [\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-\-ids\fR]"
Open notifications: on screen (newest first), waiting for room, held back by pause.
.B \-\-ids
prints the id, a tab, the app and the summary, for scripts and completion.
.TP
.BR history " [\fB\-n\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR | \fB\-\-pick\fR]"
The last
.I n
closed notifications (default 20, 0 for all), oldest first;
.B \-\-pick
chooses one in
.BR fzf (1)
and shows it again.
.TP
.BR forget " \fIid\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR"
Remove a notification from the history, or clear it.
.TP
.BR center " [\fBtoggle\fR|\fBshow\fR|\fBhide\fR]"
The notification center: a full\-height panel with a do\-not\-disturb toggle, a mode picker
(normal and the modes rules match on), a clear button, what a media player plays (MPRIS, with
previous, play/pause and next), the volume and mute of the default output (pactl), the open
notifications and the history (click one to show it again, \(mu to forget it).
SIGUSR1 toggles it too, as it did deadd's.
While it is open it takes the keyboard:
.BR j / k
(or the arrows) move between cards,
.BR g / G
to the first and last,
.B Enter
acts like a left click,
.BR d / Delete
closes or forgets one,
.B C
clears,
.B e
shows the whole body of the selected card,
.B i
shows its details (app, icon, sender, rules, times),
.B a
switches the history between grouped by app and one list,
.B ?
or
.B Ctrl+/
shows all the keys in a small window (any key closes it),
.B p
toggles do not disturb,
.B m
picks the next mode,
.BR space / n / N
play\-pause, next and previous,
.BR + / \-
change the volume, and
.BR Esc / Ctrl+[ / q
(or
.BR center.toggle\-key ,
default <Super>grave, the window manager's toggle binding the grab swallows)
close it.
.TP
.BR pop " [\fIid\fR]"
Show a closed notification again: the last one in the history, or
.IR id ,
with its icon, image (a thumbnail of image-data, kept under $XDG_CACHE_HOME/notifyd/thumbs) and sender, so rules match it again; their commands and sounds do not run again.
.TP
.BR close " [\fIid\fR] [\fB\-\-app\fR \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-summary\fR \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-body\fR \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-category\fR \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-urgency\fR \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-stack\-tag\fR \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-desktop\-entry\fR \fItext\fR]"
Close a notification, or every open one (shown, waiting or held back) whose fields all match: text,
or a /regex/ as in rules. Prints how many closed.
.TP
.BR action " [\fIid\fR [\fIkey\fR]]"
Invoke an action: the key given, else the default action, else the first one. Without an
.IR id ,
the default action of the newest notification on screen that has one, or exactly one action of its
sender's (notifyd's own buttons do not count): bind it to a key to answer the last popup, e.g. open the
Claude Code session it names. Exits 1 when no notification on screen has one.
.TP
.BI reply " id text"
Answer a notification that takes a reply (an
.B inline\-reply
action): emits NotificationReplied and dismisses it, as the center's reply field does
.RB ( r
on a card focuses the field, Enter sends, Esc leaves it).
.TP
.B close\-all
Close everything shown or waiting.
.TP
.BR pause ", " resume ", " toggle " [\fB\-\-for\fR \fIduration\fR]"
Hold back all but critical notifications; resume shows what was held back. The paused state and what is held back survive a restart of the daemon.
.B \-\-for
(30m, 1h, 90s; at most 30 days) resumes on its own after that long, also across a restart; a plain pause or resume
replaces it.
.TP
.BR mode " [\fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-for\fR \fIduration\fR]"
Show the mode, or set it;
.B \-\-for
switches back to the mode before it after that long. Rules match on it with
.BR "match: {mode: \fIname\fB}" ,
e.g. to suppress all but critical notifications in a
.B dnd
mode; the default mode is
.BR normal .
.TP
.B reload
Reload the config file; exits 1 with the error when it is broken.
.TP
.BI preset " name"
Switch the preset until the next reload.
.TP
.BR preview " [\fIpreset\fR]"
Show sample notifications (low, song with tags, volume bar, actions, critical) in a preset.
.TP
.BR bar " [\fB\-\-follow\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR] [\fB\-\-idle\fR \fItext\fR] [\fB\-\-offline\fR \fItext\fR]"
A status line for polybar and the like:
.I fmt
(default
.BR "{paused} {mode} {held}" ;
also
.B {shown}
and
.BR {missed} ,
the notifications that expired unclicked since the center was last opened)
when paused, in a mode other than normal, holding notifications back, or (with
.B {missed}
in it) having missed some, else the
.B \-\-idle
text (empty), and the
.B \-\-offline
text when notifyd is not running.
.B \-\-follow
prints a new line whenever it changes (polybar's
.BR "tail = true" ).
.TP
.BR watch " [\fB\-\-json\fR]"
Follow the daemon's event log.
.SS presets
List the presets with their descriptions.
.B \-\-pick
chooses one in
.BR fzf (1),
previewing each as the cursor moves and switching to it on enter;
.B \-\-names
prints the names only.
.SS icons
List the icons of the icon theme in use (GTK's, as the popups draw them; without a display, from
.I ~/.config/gtk\-3.0/settings.ini
or gsettings), each with its context (Status, Devices, Applications, ...). A
.I pattern
keeps names containing it, or matching it as a glob
.RB ( * ,
.BR ? );
case is ignored. Any name works as an app icon
.RB ( \-i ),
a picture
.RB ( \-\-image )
or a rule's
.BR icon.name .
.TP
.BR \-\-context " \fIname\fR"
Only that context;
.B \-\-contexts
lists them with how many icons each has.
.TP
.BR \-\-theme " \fIname\fR"
Another installed theme.
.TP
.B \-\-names
Names only, one per line.
.TP
.B \-\-pick
Browse in
.BR fzf (1):
the preview pane draws the icon with
.BR chafa (1)
and names its file and sizes, each move also shows it in a popup (when a daemon runs), enter
prints the name and ctrl\-y copies it.
.TP
.BR \-\-preview " \fIname\fR, " \-\-size " \fIpx\fR"
What the picker's preview shows, for one icon, drawn at
.I px
(64).
.PP
The daemon logs a warning, once per name or path per run (a reload counts again), for an icon or
picture that does not load: no such file, not an image, or an icon the theme lacks (with close
names). An unknown name is usually a different spelling in this theme:
.B notifyd icons
finds it.
.SS import
.B notifyd import dunst
converts
.I ~/.config/dunst/dunstrc
(or
.BR \-\-rc ):
geometry, font, frame, urgency colors (with its transparency) and timeouts, idle threshold, mouse
buttons, and its rule sections (globs become regexes; skip_display, history_ignore, script,
new_icon, set_stack_tag and colors carry over), on top of the
.B dunst
preset.
.PP
.B notifyd import deadd
converts
.I ~/.config/deadd/deadd.yml
(or
.BR \-\-yml )
and the
.I deadd.css
next to it (or
.BR \-\-css )
and prints the config, or writes it with
.BR \-o / \-\-output
.I path
(\fB\-o config\fR for the default config file;
.B \-\-force
overwrites). Style values that equal the deadd preset are left out; notes on what has no
equivalent go to standard error.
.SS check
Validate the config, including icon.name and image.path settings that would not draw (in the
config and its rules). Exits 1 when there are warnings, 2 on errors.
.SS rules
Show which rules match a notification and what it would get:
.BR \-\-app ,
.BR \-\-summary ,
.BR \-\-body ,
.BR \-\-urgency " low|normal|critical,"
.BI \-\-timeout " ms"
(what the sender asks for) and
.BI \-\-hint " name\fB=\fIvalue"
(repeatable), plus
.BR \-c / \-\-config .
.SS completion
Print a completion script for
.BR zsh ,
.B bash
or
.BR fish .
Preset names complete live from the installed presets.
.SS help, version
.B notifyd help
.RI [ command ...]
prints the help of notifyd or a command (e.g.
.BR "notifyd help ctl history" );
.BR "notifyd version" ,
.B \-V
and
.B \-\-version
print the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
YAML; every key is optional and an empty file gives the deadd look. The main keys:
.BR preset ,
.B style
(overrides of the preset's keys),
.BR width ,
.B timeout
(8000, 8s, 1500ms, 2m or never),
.BR timeout\-critical ,
.BR max\-visible ,
.B stack\-duplicates
(an identical notification replaces the open one and is counted),
.B group\-by
(a list of app, summary, category, urgency: a new notification with the same values replaces the
open one and is counted),
.BR position.anchor / margin / gap / follow / monitor
(follow: mouse, focus or none; monitor: an index or an output name such as DP\-1),
.BR icon.size / name ,
.BR image.size / path / margin\-right ,
.BR title.max\-lines ,
.BR body.max\-lines ,
.B markup
(full, strip, plain),
.B fullscreen
(show, delay: wait while the focused window is fullscreen),
.B indicate\-waiting
(+N on the last popup while N wait),
.B timeout\-bar
(true: a bar drains with the timer),
.B ignore\-close
(true: the app's own CloseNotification is ignored),
.BR lock.pause / processes
(hold everything back while the screen is locked: logind LockedHint or a locker process),
.BR hide ,
.BR class ,
.BR skip ,
.B sound
(a file or sound name; false silences),
.B buttons
(a list of
.BR "{label: \fItext\fB, run: \fIcommand\fB}" ),
.B actions.drop
(the sender's actions left out, by key or label, as text or /regex/),
.B copy\-code
(a Copy button for a one-time code in the text, default true),
.B copy\-code\-clear
(the copied code leaves the clipboard after this long if still there, default 1m; never keeps it),
.B run
(a command for each match),
.B run\-on
(when it runs: any of receive, action, close),
.B filter
(a command that rewrites it before it shows: it prints a JSON object of summary, body, icon,
urgency, progress, hints or skip),
.B filter\-timeout
(how long it may take, default 500ms; then it shows unchanged),
.B idle\-threshold
(no expiry while the user is away that long),
.BR center.width / side / hide\-on\-leave / mpris / volume ,
.BR center.modes / cards / keyboard / remote\-art / toggle\-key ,
.B center.mpris\-ignore
(player names the now-playing row passes over),
.B center.group
(history grouped by app, collapsed to each app's newest card),
.B center.buttons
(a row of {label, run, state, icon} buttons; on while state exits 0),
.BR fade.in / out
(durations such as 150ms; out defaults to 150ms, since picom does not fade translucent windows out),
.BR history.size / keep / persist ,
.BR log.format / color / level / file ,
.B click.left / middle / right
(dismiss, dismiss\-all, default\-action, none, or
.BI "exec: " command ,
which runs it and dismisses) and
.BR rules .
.PP
A rule is a
.B match
plus any of those keys except the daemon-wide ones (max\-visible, indicate\-waiting, log,
click, center, lock, rules), applied in order to the notifications it matches. A rule's
.B position
(anchor, margin, gap, follow, monitor) puts them in a stack of their own, with its own
.B max\-visible
room:
.PP
.RS
.nf
rules:
  \- name: songs
    match: { app: [musicman, MPD, Spotify] }
    timeout: 12s
    hide: [app\-icon, app\-name]
  \- match: { summary: \(aq/(?P<pct>\ed+)% battery/i\(aq }
    urgency: critical
    summary: \(aqBattery at {pct}%\(aq
.fi
.RE
.PP
Match fields are app, summary, body, icon, category, desktop\-entry, stack\-tag, urgency, mode,
process and parent (the sending process and its parent, as
.B ctl watch
shows them after "via"), fullscreen (true when the focused window was fullscreen) and
.BI hint: " {name: value}" ;
a value is an exact string, a
.BI / regex /
(with
.B i
to ignore case) or a list. Rules can also rewrite summary, body and app (templates with the
regex's named groups), urgency and stack\-tag. A rule's timeout wins over the sender's.
.PP
Presets are YAML files with a
.B style
mapping: colors, radius, border, padding, fonts per part (title, title-end, body, appname, time), urgency
colors, progress bar, buttons, raw CSS, and named
.B classes
that notification text can use as
.BR "<span class=\(dqaccent\(dq>" .
Bundled: card, catppuccin\-latte, catppuccin\-mocha, deadd, dracula, dunst, gruvbox, high\-contrast,
kanagawa, minimal, nord, one\-dark, rose\-pine, solarized\-light, swaync, tokyonight, xfce\-retro.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/notifyd/config.yaml
The config.
.TP
.I ~/.config/notifyd/presets/*.yaml
Your presets; they shadow bundled ones of the same name.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/notifyd/history.jsonl
History kept across restarts (with
.BR history.persist ,
the default).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/notifyd/state.json
The mode, the paused state and the notifications held back by pause, kept across restarts.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/notifyd/send\-ids.json
Ids for
.BR "notifyd send \-\-replace\-name" .
.PP
Commands from
.B run
and
.B buttons
run with
.BR sh (1)
in the background, with the notification as
.BR NOTIFYD_ID ,
.BR NOTIFYD_APP ,
.BR NOTIFYD_SUMMARY ,
.BR NOTIFYD_BODY ,
.BR NOTIFYD_URGENCY ,
.BR NOTIFYD_CATEGORY ,
.BR NOTIFYD_ICON ,
.B NOTIFYD_STACK_TAG
and
.B NOTIFYD_RULES
in the environment and as JSON on standard input.
.B NOTIFYD_EVENT
says why it runs (receive, action, close, click), with
.B NOTIFYD_ACTION
(the action's key),
.B NOTIFYD_CLOSE_REASON
(expired, dismissed, closed, undefined) or
.B NOTIFYD_BUTTON
(left, middle, right) for those events.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config directory is (default
.IR ~/.config ).
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
No colors in the log or in
.BR "ctl watch" / history .
.TP
.BR DISPLAY ", " DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
The X display to draw on and the session bus.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the config.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Toggle the notification center.
.TP
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Close all notifications and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when the daemon cannot own the bus name or draw, a ctl command fails, or
.B check
finds warnings; 2 for usage and config errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
notifyd daemon \-\-replace
notifyd send \-a backdrop \-\-image ~/pic.jpg \(aqNew wallpaper\(aq \(aq<i>ocean</i>\(aq
notifyd send \-\-tag volume \-\-progress 60 \-a Volume \(aqVolume 60%\(aq
notifyd send \-A \(aqchange=Change layout\(aq \(aqMonitors changed\(aq
notifyd ctl watch
notifyd presets \-\-pick
notifyd import deadd \-o config
notifyd import dunst > dunst\-converted.yaml
notifyd ctl pop
notifyd completion zsh > ~/.local/share/zsh/site-functions/_notifyd
.fi
.SH COMPLIANCE
.B tools/compliance.py
in the source tree checks the protocol behavior of whichever daemon owns the bus (replace ids,
stack tags, NotificationClosed reasons, expiry, capabilities);
.B just compliance
compares notifyd, dunst and deadd side by side.
.PP
deadd's control messages are understood: a notification with the hint
.B deadd\-notification\-center
(true or 1) and a
.B type
hint of pausePopups, unpausePopups, clearPopups,
clearInCenter (forget the history) or reloadStyle (reload the config) is carried out and logged, never
shown or kept.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR notify\-send (1),
.BR dunst (1),
.BR picom (1),
.BR fzf (1).
The full config reference is
.I docs/config.md
in the source tree.
